A1. This goes a long way on a swallow. ~ CAMEL
A5. Go to Berlin for this. ~ SONG
A8. A relative. ~ AUNT
A12. These kind of liquors are hard to find. ~ AGED
A13. When Nero played—there was a hot time in this town. ~ ROME
A14. Business is binding at this place. ~ ALTAR
A15. A women's get-together. ~ SALE
A16. How young love feels when approaching Horizontal 14. ~ ELATED
A18. Selfness. ~ EGO
A19. A red-hot place. ~ KILN
A20. A pronoun. ~ HER
A21. What America is—now that we have prohibition. ~ WET
A23. A stop signal. ~ NO
A24. A possessive pronoun. ~ HIS
A25. The wise owl does this by day. ~ REPOSES
A29. What Scotchmen do with their old clothes. ~ WEAR
A31. A western mount. ~ MUSTANG
A32. Lindy and plane, objectively. ~ US
A34. These are designed to keep you from skidding. ~ TREADS
A36. A reckless kind of devil. ~ DARE
A38. One of the best calling cards. ~ ACE
A40. A Greek letter. ~ PSI
A41. Girls letting their hair grow are recovering this. ~ EAR
A44. Neck and neck. ~ PET
A45. What the wise girl keeps her love in. ~ DARK
A47. A little boy's nemesis. ~ SISTER
A50. Part of a round trip. ~ TO
A51. Lizzie's big brother. ~ LINCOLN
A54. Razorback by-product. ~ LARD
A56. To put over a fast one. ~ DECEIVE
A57. Things aren't so sweet when this doesn't choose to run. ~ SAP
A58. Part of the verb to be. ~ AM
A60. A period of time. ~ ERA
A61. This is good to make a record with. ~ PEN
A62. A school of preparation. ~ PREP
A64. A slight recognition. ~ NOD
A67. A spirit once found in decanters. ~ CLARET
A69. Where we want to go in vacation time. ~ AWAY
A70. To beg for. ~ CRAVE
A72. A sucker is often found in this. ~ SODA
A73. A poor fish would be poor indeed without this. ~ GILL
A74. What the tide does half of the time. ~ EBBS
A75. Cleo selected one of these to end it all. ~ ASPS
A76. Horses and some men have this. ~ SENSE

D1. A large wine holder. ~ CASK
D2. Encore. ~ AGAIN
D3. What a good cigar should be. ~ MELLOW
D4. The first summer-garden to be padlocked. ~ EDEN
D5. These were never saved by walking home. ~ SOLES
D6. A very famous wet. ~ OMAR
D7. This is good for a catch. ~ NET
D8. Another famous wet. ~ AL
D9. An Indian tribe. ~ UTE
D10. This has pretty well lost its pull. ~ NAG
D11. A lively horse does this when he steps out. ~ TROTS
D13. What the big business man, who fired his too-pretty stenographer, does when his wife leaves town. ~ REHIRES
D14. This kind of a man is an expert. ~ ADEPT
D17. Little lambs' mammas. ~ EWES
D22. Plenty of hops in this. ~ TOAD
D24. You have to pull the strings to get anything out of this. ~ HARP
D25. Misleading actions. ~ RUSES
D26. This has ginger. ~ SNAP
D27. A plumed heron. ~ EGRET
D28. Slang for quadrangle. ~ QUAD
D30. A Latin conjunction. ~ ET
D31. This fellow knows his pills. (Init.) ~ MD
D33. This could take the measure of Jack Dempsey. ~ SCALE
D35. This is the way to Horizontal 14. ~ AISLE
D37. A famous English school. ~ ETON
D39. A Scandinavian navigator. ~ ERIC
D42. A southern home for bootleggers. ~ ATLANTA
D43. What you'll do if you sow wild oats. ~ REAP
D46. This joint is always being exposed. ~ KNEE
D48. Where to go when it rains. ~ IN
D49. This can't go far in Rhode Island. ~ RR
D52. An ancient enchantress. ~ CIRCE
D53. Egg shaped. ~ OVAL
D55. A man who said Adam and Eve weren't all they were once supposed to be. ~ DARWIN
D56. No holds are barred in this nowadays. ~ DANCE
D57. What some birds like to have for dinner. ~ SEEDS
D59. These will take away your appetite. ~ MEALS
